ew way to make the first  black holes so traditionally black holes  are expected tiny black holes are left  as remnants of stars but they're too  tiny to really explain how they could  account for the supermassive black holes  that inhabit the centres of most if not  all galaxies and we believe that these  black holes could have formed need to  have formed very very early on in the  universe because we're detecting these  quasars that are powered by the  supermassive black holes further and  further back in time and so the black  holes that power these are about a  million times the mass of the Sun and  you need such a big black hole to have  formed when very few large galaxies have  formed right so very few massive things  in the universe have formed and so we  proposed an AI this channel to form the  first black hole which is called direct  collapse black holes in which you bypass  the formation of a star and a lot of gas  just condenses it's rather like the  vortex when you pull the plug in your  bathtub the way the water rushes in  something similar to that happens in the  very early universe with gas and gas  gets funneled in and forms a black hole  so this picture  idea was we proposed at about 15 years  ago and you know we've been working on  it developing it and providing  observational signatures calculating  them in the James Webb Space Telescope  which was due to fly this month and has  been delayed for two years is poised to  discover them if they're out there so I  think in the near term in the next two  to three years when this telescope flies  I would be thrilled if these objects  were discovered of course I mean it  would be great either way but you know I  would be really thrilled if these red  claps black holes are actually found and  I think that they have very clear  signatures and so we will be able to  discriminate them if we find them so  that's the narottam and the longer term  is of course you know the collisions of  stellar-mass black holes these tiny  black holes was discovered by the LIGO  collaboration so the sort of earthquakes  in space-time that were detected  gravitational waves were detected by the  LIGO collaboration they were awarded the  Nobel Prize last year but you know these  are the little cousins of the black  holes that I really like the  supermassive ones they also collide in  the universe and to detect the waves the  gravitational waves that are produced  due to their collision you actually need  the kind of a paradis you had on the  ground here in space so that's another  Space Telescope called Lisa  it's a project I'm involved in on the  NASA Lisa team and that you know is  proposed to fly in the 2030s and they  will discover so this instrument will  discover the first gravitational wave  output from colliding supermassive black  holes I would love to see that I've  worked on various aspects of you know  what else you could see along with  gravitational waves from these black  holes so and you know of course there's  always this big open question right the  nature of dark matter it would be  awesome if there was some discovery of  the particle some hint even a small hint  of what that particle might be because  we know so much about how the dark  matter is distributed  how it clumps and you know how it  evolves over time and it's in the  driving seat of everything we know in  cosmology but the particle remains  elusive said that would be a very nice  discovery you know I always say maybe in  the next five years and I think we have  to be optimistic because with science  you never know you could never predict  the future course of science right I  mean these this team the LIGO team that  detected gravitational waves the first  detection they'd been working for 40  years right so let's hope it's not  another 40 year ice but we should be  pushing it for me but it would be  fabulous if you know these three things  got found illuminated understood in my  lifetime right well this has been  fascinating for me thank you so much for  being here today and sharing your work  thank you so much for having me for more  information about professor nutty Jean  and her research please visit our  website at macmillan report yale.edu be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you
 

